#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One usage table across every coding harness: Kiro, l3m, Claude Code, Codex, ...

Each harness records something different, and no one source reads them all:

  * Claude Code / Codex / opencode log REAL token counts, which this script now
    reads natively (it used to shell out to ccusage, which re-parsed every log
    -- gigabytes -- on each run and priced unknown models at a silent $0). The
    parsers transcribe ccusage's own rules from its Rust adapters and were
    validated token-for-token against its output; the differences are that
    results are cached incrementally (only logs that changed since the last run
    are re-parsed) and that pricing falls back to an embedded snapshot instead
    of $0 when LiteLLM lacks a model.

  * l3m (~/CodebaseQUENNV/.../l3m) hardcodes published Bedrock/Anthropic list
    prices as a dated, Lean-proof-pinned snapshot (cents/1M, split
    in/out/cache-read/cache-write). Narrower than LiteLLM but curated to cover
    this box's frontier models.

  * Kiro exposes no tokens at all -- only credits, which it writes per turn to
    its own session log. The $/credit applied to them is derived
    (credits->tokens->dollars, calibrate.py and FINDINGS.md), anchored to those
    same published prices, and carries a wide -13%/+88% band because the token
    count and the input/output mix are unmeasured. The credit COUNT, separately,
    is calibrated against `/usage` -- the one authoritative figure Kiro
    publishes -- because the log scan misses about 5% of turns.

So the trustworthy quantity is always (real tokens) x (published list price).
For Claude/Codex/opencode the tokens are logged, so cost is near-exact. Two
price tables exist for them: `--rates l3m` (default) prices the Claude family
from l3m's proof-pinned snapshot and everything else from the LiteLLM table;
`--rates litellm` prices everything from the LiteLLM table alone. Since both
tables now transcribe the same published list prices, the two modes usually
agree; the flag survives so a divergence between the snapshots is visible.

Kiro is the one harness with no token ground truth, so its column is credits and
its dollar figure is the only true *estimate* here -- the -13%/+88% band rides on
the Kiro subtotal alone; token costs are shown as points. Its rows come from
`~/.kiro/sessions/<workspace>/sess_<uuid>/messages.jsonl`, where each completed
turn appends a `usage_summary` record carrying that turn's credits; the model is
joined on from the `assistant` records sharing the turn's `executionId`, so a
session that switched models still attributes per turn.

l3m appears here twice, in two unrelated roles: as a price TABLE above, and --
since it burns Bedrock tokens of its own -- as a harness in its own right. It
keeps no Claude-Code-shaped token log the scanners above could read, so its
rows are read straight out of l3m's own bookkeeping: every turn boundary
publishes the session sidecar (`last_state.json` -- cumulative `cents` plus
per-wire token counters) to `refs/agents/state/<agent>/self` in the hub, so
that ref's commit history is a
dated series of running totals, and differencing consecutive samples gives daily
usage. Cost comes from l3m's own `cents`, not from re-pricing here: l3m charges
each call at the model that call actually used, which the flattened per-wire
counters can no longer tell us. So `--rates` does not move the l3m rows, and the
`Model` cell is the session's brain -- a `consult_model` side call to a different
model folds into its parent's row.

One l3m row can carry no date (`-`): the earliest sample in a ref's history is a
conversation that was already running, so that much spend is real but the days it
happened on were never recorded. It is carried undated rather than dumped on the
day it first became visible, and it survives `--since`/`--until` like any undated
row -- dropping it would silently shrink the total.

Columns: Date | Harness | Model | Consumed | Unit | Rate | CR | CW | Std | Eff |
Cost (USD), where Consumed x Rate = Cost on every row. `Unit` is `credits` for
Kiro (metered) and `Mtok` (millions of tokens) for the token-logged harnesses --
the two are not commensurable, so `Consumed` sums only within a harness;
`Cost (USD)` is the one column comparable and summable across all of them.

`Rate` is the EFFECTIVE blended $/Mtok, i.e. cost / total tokens. It is a mix
statistic, not a price, and it is not comparable across harnesses: within a
single model the list prices span 50x (opus-5 cache-read $0.50/Mtok against
output $25.00/Mtok), so a 95%-cached harness and a 75%-cached one show very
different $/Mtok under IDENTICAL pricing. Do not try to reconcile them -- both
price tables here already agree exactly, and the spread is real.

To make the comparison valid instead, four columns expose and then fix the mix.
`CR` and `CW` are the row's cache-read and cache-write shares, MEASURED from the
logs for every token harness -- no assumption anywhere. Watch CW rather than CR:
cache-write costs 1.25x fresh input, 12.5x a cache read, so a few percent of it
outweighs a large swing in CR. That is why claude-fable-5 at 80% CR / 20% CW
reads $3.41/Mtok while claude-opus-5 at 96%/4% reads $0.75. `Std` re-prices the
row at the POOLED mix of every token-logged row in the window, so it varies only
by model; `Eff` = Rate/Std reads how favourable that row's own mix was (<1.00
cheaper than this box's average, >1.00 dearer) -- codex's gpt-5.4 at Eff 4.78 is
not an expensive model, it is a badly-cached one. A row whose model no price
table lists gets no Std.

Kiro is the only harness that ASSUMES a mix rather than measuring one, because it
logs no buckets. Its CR/CW cells therefore show the mix its $/credit was derived
from, prefixed `~` so they never read as measurements: the default is `~100%`/
`~0%`, i.e. $0.0747 = $0.7468 (fresh-input basis) x 0.100 asserting that every
token was a cache read. Showing that is the point -- an assumed 100% is a claim,
and a blank cell hides it.

Kiro gets an Eff but no Std, and that asymmetry is deliberate. Std is a $/Mtok,
so filling it would mean synthesizing a token count from k and the per-model
multiplier -- an invented quantity in a column that elsewhere holds a measured
one. Eff needs nothing invented: for one model the absolute prices cancel out of
Rate/Std, leaving a ratio of MIX FACTORS, and Kiro's assumed mix is a factor
already. So its `~0.62` says its assumption prices context 38% below what the
token-logged harnesses actually achieve -- and `--kiro-mix 95/5` driving that to
`~0.98` is the check that Claude Code's measured mix is what it claims to be.
An explicit `--credit-rate` corresponds to no stated mix, so those rows show
neither: `-` in CR, CW and Eff alike.

Kiro credits are separately calibrated: the session-log scan is measurably
incomplete against `/usage`, Kiro's own authoritative total, so credits are
scaled by KIRO_COMPLETENESS (x1.0533 as recorded, ~+5%). That corrects the SCAN,
not the price -- the -13%/+88% band still rides on the $/credit conversion alone.
Use `--raw-credits` for the uncorrected scan and `--verify-credits` to re-measure.

Usage:
  ccusage-all                     # daily table, l3m rates, Kiro calibrated+estimated
  ccusage-all --rates litellm     # price everything from the LiteLLM table
  ccusage-all --kiro-mix 95/5     # price Kiro at Claude Code's measured mix
  ccusage-all --credit-rate .056  # output-heavy end of the Kiro estimate
  ccusage-all --raw-credits       # Kiro credits exactly as scanned, uncalibrated
  ccusage-all --since 2026-08-01 --verify-credits 66425.17   # re-check the scan
  ccusage-all --since 2026-08-01 --until 2026-08-14
  ccusage-all --json              # machine-readable rows + totals + reference mix
  ccusage-all --online            # refresh LiteLLM prices over the network
"""

